  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nRest of today into Monday:\n\nMorning synoptic/RAP analysis indicates ridge centered just over\nthe FL Peninsula, while another ridge is centered over the Four \nCorners. The Gulf Coast region remains on the western periphery of\nthe former mentioned ridge. High moisture laden atmospheric \nprofiles (around 2.2-2.5 inches) and a stationary frontal \nboundary situated southeast of the Natchez Trace. This is keeping\nwidespread clouds and convection initiation (CI) less robust. \nExpect low to medium rain chances and isolated to scattered (20 \nto 60 percent) while high rain chances and widespread coverage\nsoutheast of the Natchez Trace (greater than 60 to 90 percent \ncoverage). While some storms cannot be ruled out across the area,\nweak skinny thermo profiles and meager lapse rates will limit \nconvective organization again today. HREF PMMs indicate some low \nchance probs (around 10 to 30 percent) of exceeding 3 inch \nrainfall totals, mainly in east-southeast Mississippi in the\nHighway 45 to Interstate 59 corridors. However, with low\nconfidence of high convective rates, especially further south and\neast where heating will be less, holding off any advertisement in\nthe in local HWO graphics. Locally heavy downpours and isolated\nflash flooding threat cannot be ruled out again where\nbackbuilding can occur (light backbuilding flow in the Highway 84\nand 98 corridors into the Interstate 59 corridors). Temperatures \nwill be seasonable today, some 3-5F above in the Highway 82 \ncorridor (82-85F) while near normal to the south (79-81F).\n\nRain chances decrease in coverage this evening, in the low to medium\nchance and scattered coverage (25 to 55 percent), with areas of\nthe highest chances southeast of the Natchez Trace likely in the \nPine Belt through daybreak Sunday. Lows will seasonably warm, some\n6-12F above (62-68F). Deep trough over the Great Lakes to Hudson\nBay and shortwave over the ArkLaTex will drive a drying front down\ninto the area into early this week. Deep moisture decrease from\nnorth to south through the day, with low to medium chance (15 to\n30 percent) of rain southeast of the Natchez Trace. Temperatures \nwill be seasonably warm, some 4-8F above (82-88F), with areas\nnorthwest of the Natchez Trace warmer as areas to the northwest\nwill be mostly sunny.\n\nThrough next weekend...\n\nHigh pressure will establish northwest flow aloft and low-level\neasterlies over the area which will push a backdoor front into \nthe area. This now looks to be the main push of reinforcing dry \nair into the area into midweek. This will keep a dry but gradually\nmoderating pattern ongoing into late week. Main attention will\nturn to the Bay of Campeche and southwest Gulf, where numerous AI\nmodel suite consensus and now operational GFS indicate some\norganized low pressure and potential tropical development late \nthrough the week into next weekend. The National Hurricane Center \nhas added a 40 percent chance of development in this region next \nweek. The ensemble mean track is generally meander in the Bay of \nCampeche before potential northwest to north propagation. There may \nbe some influence of an upper low diving into the southern Plains \nand stout upper jet diving into the Rio Grande and International \nBorder into the Gulf Coast states. This could keep the system more \nsheared and asymmetric, which would focus impacts along and east of \nany potential track. At the very least, there is enough to \nnecessitate monitoring for some potential slug of tropical moisture \ninto late week and next weekend. Seasonable warmth will turn to more \nseasonable temperatures into next weekend. /DC/SAS/",
